        "text": "This pronunciation uses a long O, hard G, and silent E \u2014 the reasoning being that the original word \"dog\" has a hard G, so the E only modifies the vowel, not the consonant, sort of like how \"slop\" becomes \"slope\" and \"mop\" becomes \"mope.\"",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n### 3. \"Doh-geh\" (Two Syllables)\n**Sounds like:** *dog-ay*\n\n",
